01:03:42,222 --> 01:03:48,222
Olympia competitions and during the competitions themselves, the filmmakers ran
out of funds to finish production and it stalled for two years.

21
01:06:53,333 --> 01:06:59,333
Ultimately, Schwarzenegger and other bodybuilders featured in the film helped
raise funds to complete production, and it was released in 1977.

22
01:10:04,444 --> 01:10:10,444
The film became a box office success, making Schwarzenegger a household name.

23
01:13:15,555 --> 01:13:21,555
The film also served to popularize the culture of bodybuilding, which was somewhat niche at the time, and helped inspire the fitness
craze of the 1980s; following the film's release, there was a marked increase in the number of commercial gyms in the U.S.

24
01:16:26,666 --> 01:16:32,666
The film was released on CED and VHS, and then re-released on DVD in
2003 for the 25th anniversary of the theatrical debut.

25
01:19:37,777 --> 01:19:43,777
The film inspired four sequels: George Butler's Pumping Iron II: The Women (1985), a documentary about the world of female bodybuilding; Mark Nalley's Stand Tall (1997), a documentary that features Lou Ferrigno's return to bodybuilding after 19 years; David and Scott
McVeigh's Raw Iron (2002), a documentary about the making of Pumping Iron and how the film affected the lives of those who appeared in it; and Vlad Yudin's Generation Iron (2013), a documentary on which Pumping Iron producer Jerome Gary served as executive producer.

